+# Privacy and third-party audit
+
+Reviewed November 11, 2026. Scope: the static pages and assets in this repository.
+
+## Data minimization and client-side behavior
+
+The site ships no client JavaScript, forms, analytics, tracking pixels, third-party
+fonts, embeds, cookies, browser storage, or service workers. No consent record or
+visitor identifier is created. Keep browsing anonymous; a consent banner would
+introduce unnecessary state when there is no optional tracking.
+
+The original Unsplash CSS background contacted images.unsplash.com on each visit.
+The same stock photograph is now stored as assets/coffee.jpg and served locally.
+Source: https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085
+No external asset fetch is needed during a build or visitor session.
+
+## Third-party inventory
+
+| Component | Visitor data exposure | Review / decision |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Unsplash image | None to Unsplash after local hosting | Static asset only; no remote URL in public markup or CSS. Review license and attribution obligations before replacing assets. |
+| Hosting/CDN provider | IP address, request URL, user agent and other connection metadata may be visible | Provider is not specified in this repository. Operator must review logging, retention, subprocessors, access and deletion settings before launch. |
+| statikk via pnpm dlx | Development tooling can contact the package registry | Existing start command uses an unpinned download. Do not use it as production hosting; deploy dist/ using the configurations provided. |
+| Runtime packages / third-party scripts | None | No production dependencies and no browser scripts. The build uses only Node built-ins. |
+
+Repeat this review when introducing scripts, services, assets, dependencies,
+collection fields or account features. Record purpose, minimum fields, recipients,
+retention, deletion and whether opt-in is required before enabling collection.
+
+## Security headers and deployment
+
+Run `npm run build` then `npm test`. Deploy only dist/. Netlify uses netlify.toml
+and the generated dist/_headers. Vercel uses vercel.json. Other hosts must apply
+the headers in scripts/security-policy.mjs as HTTP response headers on all routes,
+including error responses. These files do not configure arbitrary servers.
+
+The HTML CSP and referrer meta tags provide a fallback. Framing protection,
+Permissions-Policy, HSTS and other response-only protections require the host to
+apply HTTP headers; a meta tag cannot replace them. Deploy over HTTPS and redirect
+HTTP to HTTPS at the host. HSTS is scoped to the current host (no preload or
+includeSubDomains commitment). CSP blocks scripts, outbound connections, forms,
+embedded frames and plugins, while allowing only same-origin styles and images.
+No CSP reporting endpoint is configured, avoiding collection of visitor URLs.
+Responses request no cache storage; this cannot erase existing browser history
+or logs. After deployment, verify actual response headers using the hosting
+provider's tooling or an HTTP client.
+
+## Secure handling for future features
+
+Account, ordering and logout links are existing navigation placeholders; there is
+no authentication, payment service or data-processing backend here. The restrictive
+policy deliberately blocks form submissions and browser API connections. Before
+enabling these features, implement server-side validation and authorization,
+CSRF protection for state changes (including POST logout), secure HttpOnly,
+SameSite session cookies, limited retention, deletion, and payment-provider
+tokenization. Keep personal data, credentials and tokens out of URLs, browser
+storage, client bundles, analytics and logs. Never embed secrets in static files.
+Change policy permissions narrowly for a reviewed feature, and update the public
+privacy notice to match actual processing before collecting data.
+
+The public notice explicitly distinguishes the site's behavior from unspecified
+host logging. No promises of host retention limits or regulatory certification
+are made by these source changes.
